  </header>
  <main class="flex-1 w-full py-16 px-4 md:px-6">
    <!-- Article Header -->
    <header class="max-w-reading mx-auto mb-16">
      <h1
        class="font-display text-[32px] md:text-[48px] font-semibold leading-tight text-text-main mb-8"
      >
        Building Resilient Distributed Systems: A Practical Guide to Raft
      </h1>
      <div
        class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 pb-4 border-b border-border-color font-mono text-[13px] text-text-muted"
      >
        <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary">AUTHOR:</span> Alice Engineer
        </span>
        <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary">DATE:</span> 2023-10-27
        </span>
        <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-primary">READ_TIME:</span> 14m
        </span>
      </div>
    </header>
    <!-- Article Content -->
    <article
      class="max-w-reading mx-auto space-y-8 text-[18px] leading-[1.6] text-text-main"
    >
      <p>
        Consensus is the heart of any distributed system. When multiple nodes
        need to agree on a single source of truth—whether it's a database
        cluster, a configuration manager, or a distributed lock service—things
        get complicated quickly. Network partitions, node failures, and message
        delays conspire to create chaos.
      </p>
      <p>
        For years, Paxos was synonymous with distributed consensus. However,
        Paxos is famously difficult to understand and even harder to implement
        correctly. In 2013, Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout introduced <code
          class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
          >Raft</code
        >, an algorithm designed explicitly for understandability.
      </p>
      <h2
        class="font-display text-[28px] font-semibold text-text-main mt-16 mb-6"
      >
        The Anatomy of a Node
      </h2>
      <p>
        In Raft, every node in the cluster can be in one of three states: <strong
          class="text-white font-medium">Follower</strong
        >, <strong class="text-white font-medium">Candidate</strong>, or <strong
          class="text-white font-medium">Leader</strong
        >. All nodes start as Followers. If a Follower hears no communication
        from a Leader within a randomized timeout period, it transitions to a
        Candidate and initiates an election.
      </p>
      <!-- Full Bleed Code Block -->
      <div
        class="relative w-full max-w-code mx-auto -mx-4 sm:mx-auto my-12 group"
      >
        <div
          class="flex items-center justify-between bg-surface border border-b-0 border-border-color px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[13px]"
        >
          <span class="text-text-muted">src/node.rs</span>
          <button
            aria-label="Copy code"
            class="text-text-muted hover:text-primary transition-colors focus:outline-none flex items-center gap-1 group/btn"
          >
            <span
              class="material-symbols-outlined text-[16px] group-hover/btn:hidden"
              >content_copy</span
            >
            <span class="hidden group-hover/btn:inline text-primary"
              >[copy]</span
            >
          </button>
        </div>
        <div
          class="bg-surface border border-border-color overflow-x-auto p-6 scrollbar-thin scrollbar-thumb-border-color scrollbar-track-transparent"
        >
          <pre
            class="font-mono text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-text-main"
            set:html={rustCode}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The simplicity of this state machine is deceptive. The interactions
        between these states, driven by RPC calls (<code
          class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
          >RequestVote</code
        > and <code
          class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
          >AppendEntries</code
        >), are where the actual consensus is forged.
      </p>
      <h3
        class="font-display text-[24px] font-semibold text-text-main mt-12 mb-4"
      >
        Log Replication
      </h3>
      <p>
        Once a Leader is elected, it begins accepting client requests. Each
        request is a command to be executed by the replicated state machines.
        The Leader appends the command to its log as a new entry, then issues <code
          class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
          >AppendEntries</code
        > RPCs in parallel to each of the other servers to replicate the entry.
      </p>
      <!-- Image Figure -->
      <figure class="my-12">
        <div class="border border-border-color p-1 bg-surface">
          <img
            alt="Abstract representation of distributed network nodes"
            class="w-full h-auto object-cover img-grayscale max-h-[400px]"
            data-alt="Server rack network cables"
            src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/aida-public/AB6AXuAmPtpPdwSQRjE6y6xuln9JzoRnt3TaW8UJlco8WgMPIQ_1sr9RCSPQ0FUgArCE_QznTeJa9UHxW-eIQS_tYEIRNPJctvGFDqjePOoJyyUakRjHlpcyevz-tPifIsWcsjYA4uKq5S_UQ0UFLq_Qa48zK9aLO3_rRYyrlclgZmIZFHbiLcLUHMRDtsFEKw65kJ2JJ0wDk0xQzRKsOyJ5JlrLHG3R22o4KkvKLDiwpLiFhfPWbggV2kmAyNDnYZkeEk8mWZbI2ylnNzs"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption
          class="mt-4 font-mono text-[13px] text-text-muted text-center"
        >
          FIG 1. Physical manifestation of a distributed network partition.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        When the entry has been safely replicated (meaning a majority of
        followers have acknowledged it), the leader applies the entry to its
        state machine and returns the result of that execution to the client. If
        followers crash or run slowly, or if network packets are lost, the
        leader retries <code
          class="bg-surface text-primary px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-sm font-mono text-[15px] border border-border-color"
          >AppendEntries</code
        > RPCs indefinitely until all followers eventually store all log entries.
      </p>
      <!-- Terminal output block -->
      <div class="relative w-full max-w-code mx-auto -mx-4 sm:mx-auto my-12">
        <div
          class="bg-background-dark border border-border-color p-6 font-mono text-[14px] leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto text-text-muted"
        >
          <pre><code>$ cargo run --bin raft-cluster
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-1] Initializing as Follower (Term: 0)
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-2] Initializing as Follower (Term: 0)
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-3] Initializing as Follower (Term: 0)
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-2] Election timeout reached. Transitioning to Candidate.
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-2] Requesting votes for Term 1...
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-1] Granted vote to NODE-2 for Term 1
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> [NODE-3] Granted vote to NODE-2 for Term 1
<span class="text-primary">&gt;</span> <span class="text-text-main font-bold">[NODE-2] Elected Leader for Term 1. Commencing heartbeats.</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Implementing this in Rust provides unique advantages. The ownership
        model ensures that references to log entries are safe across threads,
        and the type system allows us to encode state transitions elegantly
        using enums and pattern matching. In the next section, we'll dive into
        handling network partitions.
      </p>
    </article>
    <!-- Footer / End of article marker -->
    <div class="max-w-reading mx-auto mt-24 mb-8 flex justify-center">
      <span class="font-mono text-primary">EOF</span>
    </div>
